{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent underprediction during strong uptrends: when the last 5–10 days show a clear rising regime (higher highs / higher lows), add a positive level shift to the whole path (≈ +0.5 to +2 €/t), increasing with horizon (larger boost after ~day 10).",
    "Horizon-dependent bias: near-term (days 1–7) errors are small to mildly negative; mid/long horizon (days ~15–30) become strongly negative in rally regimes. Apply an upward tilt (increasing adjustment with lead time) rather than a flat shift.",
    "Model mean-reverts too early after breakouts: following a breakout to new 30-day highs, the forecast drifts back toward mid-60s while realized prices keep trending higher. Delay mean reversion; keep prices elevated for ~2–3 weeks before allowing pullback.",
    "Underestimation of upside volatility/tail risk: realized series exhibits late-horizon upside jumps (e.g., into low/mid-70s) that the forecast caps below ~70. Add a convex ‘trend extension’ term when momentum is positive to widen the upper tail.",
    "If recent realized volatility is rising alongside positive momentum, reduce any downward corrections in the forecast path (i.e., don’t fade the move); instead, bias corrections upward and increase with volatility."
  ]
}
